{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17b866bd\" lines=\"49-67\">\n<description>\nMove each marker from a lattice corner gap into the adjacent enclosed 12-pixel cavity.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a repeated teal lattice around black 12-pixel cavities.\n- A marker is a non-black pixel placed at the northwest corner gap of one\n  complete 5x5 lattice tile; the marker may itself be teal.\n\nRule:\n- For every marked northwest corner gap, clear that marker to black.\n- Fill the 12 black pixels in the tile immediately down-right of the marker\n  with the marker color.\n- Leave the rest of the lattice unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The apparent isolated markers are control pixels for the adjacent cavity;\n  teal markers are recognized as extra teal pixels occupying a gap that the\n  periodic lattice template says should be black.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1818057f\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nDetect thin '+' shapes in a single foreground color and recolor them to teal.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One foreground color (e.g. yellow) forms various shapes on the bg, some of which are small '+' crosses.\n\nRule:\n- A '+' center is any foreground pixel whose 4 orthogonal neighbors are also foreground.\n- Recolor each such center and its 4 neighbors to teal (8).\n- All other pixels remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Only compact 5-cell crosses qualify — a pixel embedded in a larger filled region also satisfies the neighbor check, so the rule highlights every local '+' motif, not just isolated ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f341894c\" lines=\"63-78\">\n<description>\nReorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.\n- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.\n- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.\n\nRule\n- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.\n- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.\n- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"981571dc\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nReconstruct a 4-way symmetric mosaic by filling missing (black) pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A colored grid exhibiting bilateral symmetry along both a vertical and a horizontal axis.\n- Some pixels are erased to color 0 (black), creating gaps in the pattern.\n\nRule:\n- Infer the vertical and horizontal reflection axes from the visible (non-zero) pixels.\n- For each black pixel, recover its color from the mirrored position across either axis or both.\n- When multiple mirrors yield candidates, use the majority color.\n\nKey insight: The reflection axes are not given — they must be deduced from the surviving pixels by finding the axes that best preserve the existing symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"984d8a3e\" lines=\"5-21\">\n<description>\nShift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has three colors.\n- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.\n- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.\n- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.\n- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.\n- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dc46ea44\" lines=\"11-25\">\n<description>\nMove the lower sprite and its pink guide into the upper panel, aligning the sprite to the guide's lower-left branch.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one full-width separator row.\n- Below the separator are exactly two foreground roles: a five-pixel guide marker and one monochrome sprite.\n- The guide marker has normalized shape {(0,2), (1,2), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1)} and starts immediately below the separator.\n\nRule\n- Clear the canvas to the background, preserving the separator row.\n- Translate the guide marker upward so its top row becomes row 0.\n- Move the sprite, preserving its color and shape, so the sprite bbox bottom-right aligns to the translated guide's lower-left branch.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker supplies the destination anchor: its lower-left branch cell is where the moved sprite's bbox bottom-right must land.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4a1cacc2\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nFill the rectangle from the lone foreground pixel to its nearest grid corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a uniform background field with exactly one foreground pixel.\n- The foreground pixel's color is the color used for all added pixels.\n\nRule\n- Find the grid corner with the smallest Manhattan distance to the foreground pixel.\n- Recolor every pixel in the inclusive axis-aligned rectangle bounded by that\n  corner and the foreground pixel to the foreground color.\n\nKey insight\n- The single pixel is one corner of the rectangle; the opposite corner is the\n  nearest corner of the whole canvas.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ad3b40cf\" lines=\"72-87\">\n<description>\nMirror the smallest non-axis foreground color across the full straight axis.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color forms exactly one full row, column, main diagonal, or anti-diagonal reflection axis.\n- The remaining foreground colors include a unique least-populated color to copy.\n\nRule\n- Preserve the input grid.\n- Select every pixel of the unique smallest non-axis foreground color.\n- Reflect those pixels across the full straight axis and paint the reflected positions with the same color.\n\nKey insight\n- The axis color marks the mirror line, while the color population chooses which set of objects is duplicated.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
